    {
      "paperId": "ea18b72f45163540520a0268185934d08d1575bb",
      "title": "The National and the Universal",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "S. Collins",
          "authorId": "143753327"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2020,
      "abstract": "This chapter examines the continuities between the categories of the \u201cnational\u201d and the \u201cuniversal\u201d in the nineteenth century. It construes these categories as interrelated efforts to create a \u201cworld\u201d on various scales. The chapter explores the perceived role of music as a world-making medium within these discourses. It argues that the increased exposure to cultural difference and the interpretation of that cultural difference as distant in time and space shaped a conception of \u201chumanity\u201d in terms of a universal history of world cultures. The chapter reexamines those early nineteenth-century thinkers whose work became inextricably linked with the rise of exclusivist notions of nationalism in the late nineteenth century, such as Johann Gottfried Herder and John Stuart Mill. It draws from their respective treatment of music to recover their early commitment to universalizable principles and their view that the \u201cworld\u201d is something that must be actively created rather than empirically observed.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1093/OXFORDHB/9780190616922.013.20",
